{"id":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature","title":"def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature","path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature.md","line":124,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature.tex#def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature","uses":["[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator]]","[[references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature#def:pet-ch3-bivector-inner-product]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/Petersen/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch03_Curvature.tex","label":"def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature","line":230,"source":"\\begin{definition}[sectional curvature]\n  \\label{def:pet-ch3-sectional-curvature}\n\n  \\source{petersen:page-0101}\n  \\uses{def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator, def:pet-ch3-bivector-inner-product}\n\n  The \\emph{sectional curvature} of $v,w\\in T_pM$ is the normalized biquadratic form\n  \\[\n    \\sec(v,w)=\\frac{g(R_v(w),w)}{g(v,v)g(w,w)-g(v,w)^2}\n    =\\frac{g(R(w,v)v,w)}{g(v\\wedge w,v\\wedge w)}.\n  \\]\n  Since the denominator is the squared area of the parallelogram spanned by\n  $v,w$, $\\sec(v,w)$ depends only on the plane $\\pi=\\operatorname{span}\\{v,w\\}$.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[sectional curvature] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a7065742d6368332d73656374696f6e616c2d637572766174757265\"></span> <p></p><span class=\"tex-source\" style=\"display:block\">Source: <span class=\"reference\">petersen:page-0101</span></span> <a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FPetersen%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh03_Curvature%23def%3Apet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator\">↗ def:pet-ch3-directional-curvature-operator</a> <a clas